## 7. The Recursive Check: Applying the Test to \( \mathcal{P} \)

We now apply GFF-1's own test to the proposition \( \mathcal{P} \):

\[
T(\Phi, \mathcal{P}, p_{\text{examination}}, \theta) = \text{True?}
\]

Where \( p_{\text{examination}} \) is the purpose of examining the proposition.

**Ground:** \( \Phi \) (fidelity as observed)

**Derivation:** \( \mathcal{P} \) is derived from observing the structural position of \( L \) in the generative chain.

**Test:** Does \( \mathcal{P} \) faithfully represent \( \Phi \) for the purpose of examination?
- It acknowledges that logic serves purposes productively—this is faithful to observation.
- It acknowledges that logic cannot ground itself—this is faithful to Gödelian and philosophical observation.
- It does not claim more than it can deliver—it offers a structural analysis, not a proof of the proposition in logic itself.

**Acknowledgment:** \( \mathcal{P} \) carries loss. It does not capture all possible senses of "logic" or "rationality." It is an invitation to refine.

**Verdict:** \( T(\Phi, \mathcal{P}, p_{\text{examination}}, \theta) = \text{True} \) provisionally.

