## 10. Conclusion

The proposition \( \mathcal{P} \)—that logic is productive as a tool but irrational as a foundation—is not a rejection of logic. It is a proper placement of logic within a generative framework that acknowledges purposes, losses, and derivations. Logic serves us magnificently when we use it. It fails us when we worship it.

The irrationality of logic as a foundation is not a defect of logic; it is a defect of foundationalism. The ground is not logic. The ground is fidelity—observed, encountered, and served imperfectly. Logic is one of our most powerful servants. It should not be our master.

